<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyarbakir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyarbakir, the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. It was a key fortress on the frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> in 359 CE after a siege. Much later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> lost it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diyarbakir.jpg" alt="Diyarbakir" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cemallamec · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goletli_Park,_Diyarbakir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Aq Qoyunlu, or ‘White Sheep’ Turkmens, were a confederation of Oghuz Turkmen tribes led by the Bayandur clan, whose flocks pastured around <b>Diyarbakır</b> and the upper Tigris in the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Safavids permanently lost <b>Diyarbakir</b> and much of Kurdistan and eastern Anatolia, and the myth of Ismail's invincibility, on which Qizilbash commitment had rested, was shattered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It made possible Shapur I's capture of Antioch in the 250s, Shapur II's seizure of Amida (modern <b>Diyarbakir</b>) in 359 CE after a 73-day siege, and the fall of Jerusalem to Khosrow II's general Shahrbaraz in 614 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…were the Tatar journalist Yusuf Akçura, who opposed Ottomanism and supported the separation of church and state; Mehmet Cavit Bey, minister of finance, hanged for treason in 1926; Marcel Samuel Raphael Cohen (Tekin Alp), an ideologue of pan-Turkism; and the sociologist Ziya Gökalp, a Turkish nationalist from <b>Diyarbakir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hasankeyf line ruled the upper Tigris valley and from 1183, when Saladin gave them Amid, the city of <b>Diyarbakir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/artuqids-of-hasankeyf">Artuqids of Hasankeyf</a></li><li><blockquote>Aramaean groups established Bit Zamani on the upper Tigris after the Late Bronze Age collapse, with its capital at Amedi, the city that became <b>Diyarbakır</b>. It appears in Assyrian sources from the late 10th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-zamani">Bit Zamani</a></li></ul></section>
